          %h4#TheClassicalObservables The classical observables
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi loc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, Q, \langle-, -\rangle_{loc})
            a free field theory, def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo !
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi Dens
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E^! \coloneqq E^\ast \otimes Dens_X
          %p
            and accordingly write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℰ
                        %mo !
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathcal{E}^!}
            for its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distributional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          #ClassicalObservables.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              The complex of
              %strong global classical observables
              of the free field theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi loc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,Q, \langle-,- \rangle_{loc})
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+BV-complex"} classical BV-complex
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi cl
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Sym
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                      %mo !
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Obs^{cl} \coloneqq (Sym \mathcal{E}^!_c, Q)
            %p
              given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+algebra"} symmetric algebra
              of dual sections and quipped with the dual of the differential (which we denote by the same letter) defined on generators and then extended as a graded
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
              to the full
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+algebra"} symmetric algebra
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
              of local classical observables is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosheaf"} cosheaf
              of these observables which assigns to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
              the complex
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi cl
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Sym
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Obs^{cl} \colon U \mapsto (Sym \mathcal{E}^!_c(U), Q)
                    \\,.
          %p
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam
            ), this is def. 5.3.6.
          %h4#TheQuantumObservables The quantum observables
          %p
            There is a canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-quantization"} BV-quantization
            of the
            %a{:href => "#TheClassicalObservables"} above classical observable
            of a free field theory given by defining the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+Laplacian"} BV Laplacian
            as follows.
          #TheStandardBVLaplacian.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi loc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, Q, \langle -,-\rangle_{loc})
              a free field theory, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
              %strong
                standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+Laplacian"} BV Laplacian
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Sym
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                      %mo !
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sym
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta
                    \\colon
                    Sym \mathcal{E}^!_c \to Sym \mathcal{E}^!_c
            %p
              is given on generators
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Sym
                      %mn 1
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in Sym^1 \mathcal{E}^!_c
              of homogeneous degree by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta(a \cdot b) \coloneqq \{a,b\}
            %p and then extended to arbitrary elements by the formula
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi b
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi deg
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta(a \cdot b)
                    \\coloneqq
                    (\Delta a) \cdot b
                    +
                    (-1)^{deg(a)}
                    a \cdot (\Delta b)
                    +
                    \\{a,b\}
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam
            ) this is construction 2.4.9 (also construction 3.1.6, and section 5.3.3).
          #QuantumObservables.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi loc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, Q, \langle -,-\rangle_{loc})
              a free field theory, def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
              its complex of
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
              is then the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+BV-complex"} quantum BV-complex
              deformation of the classical BV-complex, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalObservables"}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheStandardBVLaplacian"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↦
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi Sym
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ℰ
                        %mi c
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo +
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Obs^{q} \colon U \mapsto
                    \\left(
                    Sym(\mathcal{E}^!_c(U))[ [ \hbar ] ], Q + \hbar \Delta
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam
            ) this is def. 5.3.9.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#characterization_of_the_quantum_observables Characterization of the quantum observables
          %p
            We characterize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+complex"} cochain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Obs
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Obs^q
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            of def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuantumObservables"}
            by an equivalent but small complex built from just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+complex"} elliptic complex
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E}, Q)
          #TheQuantumBVComplexOnTheCohomologyOfTheFieldComplex.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi loc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, Q, \langle -,-\rangle_{loc})
              a free field theory, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E}, Q, \langle -,-\rangle)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              to a graded symplectic vector space
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (H^\bullet(\mathcal{E}, \langle -,-\rangle)
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheStandardBVLaplacian"}
              there is a standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-Laplacian"} BV-Laplacian
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi H
                            %mo •
                          %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{H^\bullet\mathcal{E}}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ𝒱𝒬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mo •
                        %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{BVQ}(H^\bullet \mathcal{E})
                    \\coloneqq
                    ( Sym (H^\bullet(\mathcal{E}))^*, \Delta_{H^\bullet \mathcal{E}} )
            %p
              for the corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+BV-complex"} quantum BV-complex
          %p
            This is part of (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam, prop. 2.4.10, prop. 5.5.1
            ),
          %blockquote
            %p Handle the following with care for the moment.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For a free field theory
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mi loc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,Q,\langle-,- \rangle_{loc})
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Obs
                        %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Obs^q
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuantumObservables"}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphic
              to the BV-quantization of the cohomology of the field complex, given by def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheQuantumBVComplexOnTheCohomologyOfTheFieldComplex"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi q
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ℬ𝒱𝒬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Obs^q
                    \\simeq
                    \\mathcal{BVQ}(H^\bullet(\mathcal{E}))
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam, prop. 5.5.1
            ).
          %p
            The proof is supposedly along the lines of (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam, section 2.5
            ), applying the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+perturbation+lemma"} homological perturbation lemma
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The bracket
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
              on the complex of quantum observables
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Obs^q
              of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuantumObservables"}
              descends to a bracket on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (H^\bullet (Obs^q), \{-,-\})
              into a graded
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in Obs^q
              be closed elements of homogeneous degree. Then by the compatibly of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
              also
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\}
              is closed:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta \{a,b\}
                    \=
                    \\{\Delta a, b\} \pm \{a , \Delta b\} = 0
                    \\,.
            %p
              Let in addition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Obs
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in Obs^q
              be any element. Then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi b
                            %mo +
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo +
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo +
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi b
                            %mo −
                            %mi a
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi Δ
                              %mn 2
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo +
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\{a, b + \Delta c\}
                    \&amp;=
                    \\{a,b\} + \{a, \Delta c\}
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \{a,b\} + \Delta (a \cdot (\Delta b)) - (\Delta a)\cdot b   -a \cdot (\Delta^2 b)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \{a,b\} + \Delta (a \cdot (\Delta b))
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              and hence the cohomology class of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\}
              is independent of the representative cocycle
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#0DimensionalFreeFieldTheory 0-Dimensional free field theory
          %p
            A degenerate but instructive class of examples to compare to is the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = *
            is the 0-dimensional connected manifold: the point. (See (
            %a{:href => "#Gwilliam"}> Gwilliam 2.3.1
            )).
          %p In this case
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E = V \oplus V^*[-1]
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of a vector space and its formal dual shifted in degree. The pairing is the canonical pairing between a vector space and its dual.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^i \colon V \to \mathbb{R}\}_i
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            for functional on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi ξ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\xi_i\}
            is the corresponding basis of functions on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^*[-1]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antibracket"} antibracket
            in this case is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi ξ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ξ
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} x
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi j
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{
                  X^i, x^j
                  \\}
                  \= 0
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\{\xi_i, \xi_j\} = 0
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\{x^i, \x_j\} = \delta^i_j
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-Laplacian"} BV-Laplacian
            in this basis is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta = \sum_{i = 1}^n \frac{\partial}{\partial x^i} \frac{\partial}{\partial \xi_i}
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian+distribution"} Gaussian distribution
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            defined by a matrix
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = (a_{i j})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Q = \sum_{i,j =1}^n x^i a_{i j} \frac{\partial}{\partial \xi_i}
                  \\,.
          %p Hence for a field of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi ξ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi = \sum_{i = 1}^n \phi^i \xi_i
          %p
            we have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Q
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msup
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mrow
                              %mi k
                              %mi j
                          %msup
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %msub
                            %mi ξ
                            %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi exp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msup
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mi j
                          %msup
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\exp(S(\phi))
                  \&amp; =
                  \\exp(-\langle \phi , Q \phi\rangle)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = \exp( - \phi^i a_{k j} \phi^j \langle \xi_i, x^k   \rangle )
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = \exp(- \phi^i a_{i j} \phi^j )
                  \\end{aligned}
          %h3#locally_free_field_theories Locally free field theories
          %p
            Some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            quantum field theories have the property that they are free
            %em locally
            on their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spaces"} target spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of free field theory locally yields a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            from which the full quantization of the field theory may be reconstructed.
          %p
            A famous example of this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+twist"} topologically twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 2d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            (see there for more, and see (
            %a{:href => "\#{#Gwilliam}"} Gwilliam, section 6
            for the description in terms of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras"} factorization algebras
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian+probability+distribution"}> Gaussian probability distribution
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian+integral"} Gaussian integral
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick%27s+lemma"} Wick's lemma
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Discussion of free field theories and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+Lorentzian+manifolds"} globally hyperbolic Lorentzian manifolds
            is in
          %ul
            %li#BaerGinouxPfaeffle07
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+B%C3%A4r"}> Christian Bär
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolas+Ginoux"} Nicolas Ginoux
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Pf%C3%A4ffle"} Frank Pfäffle
              = succeed "," do
                %em Wave Equations on Lorentzian Manifolds and Quantization
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1036"} arXiv:0806.1036
          %p
            Discussion on Euclidean manifolds and in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            is in
          %ul
            %li#CostelloGwilliam
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"} Owen Gwilliam
                %em Factorization algebras in perturbative quantum field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.northwestern.edu/~costello/factorization_public.html"}> wiki
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.northwestern.edu/~gwilliam/factorization.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Gwilliam
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"}> Owen Gwilliam
                ,
                %em Factorization algebras and free field theories
                PhD thesis (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/GwilliamThesis.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"}> Owen Gwilliam
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rune+Haugseng"} Rune Haugseng
                %em Linear Batalin-Vilkovisky quantization as a functor of ∞-categories
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.01290"}> arXiv:1608.01290
                )
